1. enqueue() -> insert into queue 

void ParcelIntake::enqueue(const string& parcelID) {
    if (isQueued(parcelID)) {
        return;
    }

    QueueNode* newNode =
        new QueueNode{parcelID, nullptr};

    if (rear == nullptr) {
        front = rear = newNode;
    } else {
        rear->next = newNode;
        rear = newNode;
    }
}

1. Call isQueued() to prevent the Parcel ID from appearing twice in the queue.
2. Create a new queue node.
3. If the queue is empty, both front and rear point to the new node.
4. Otherwise, connect the old rear node to the new node.
5. Update rear to point to the new node.
This follows FIFO because new parcels are always added to the rear.

2. dequeue() -> remove from queue 

void ParcelIntake::dequeue() {
    if (isQueueEmpty()) {
        cout << "Error: Incoming queue is empty.\n";
        return;
    }

    QueueNode* temp = front;
    front = front->next;

    if (front == nullptr) {
        rear = nullptr;
    }

    delete temp;
}

1. Check whether the queue is empty.
2. Store the current front node in temp.
3. Move front to the next node.
4. If the queue becomes empty, set rear to nullptr.
5. Delete the old front node.

3. removefromqueue() -> remove a specific parcel 

This function is different from normal FIFO dequeue().

It is required when a parcel must be removed before reaching the front, such as when it is cancelled or reported missing.

The function uses two pointers, current and previous, to locate the target queue node and reconnect the surrounding nodes.

It also updates front or rear when the removed node is at either end.

Its worst-case time complexity is O(q).

