Q1. Why use both a Singly Linked List and a Queue?

The linked list maintains the full parcel records, while the queue controls processing order. Removing a parcel from the queue does not delete its record.

Q2. Why not use an array?

A linked list can grow dynamically without a predetermined capacity. However, it has additional pointer memory overhead and slower random access.

Q3. Why is FIFO appropriate?

It supports first-come, first-served intake processing, reducing the likelihood of earlier parcels being overlooked.

Q4. What is the difference between enqueue and dequeue?

Enqueue adds a parcel to the rear of the queue, while dequeue removes the parcel at the front.

Q5. What happens if the queue is empty?

The system checks the front pointer before processing, prints an error and avoids accessing an invalid node.

Q6. What happens after a parcel is processed?

Its queue node is removed, but its parcel record remains stored in the linked list with an updated status.

Q7. How does the system prevent memory leaks?

The destructor traverses and deletes dynamically allocated queue and linked-list nodes when the object is destroyed.

